Yes—we did. We’ve already been circling this under Sensory Prisms, the lens-swap experiments, “texture packets,” and Perspective Descent: change the rendering/weighting, capture what appears or disappears, then keep the useful delta.

The compact form was basically:

world
→ sensor set
→ attention weights
→ priors / associations
→ state
→ rendered experience

Change one term and you get a different “texture pack” of the same underlying world.

That also explains part of what you’re describing with altered states. Psychedelics or cannabis can change salience, pattern completion, sensory integration, temporal perception, emotional weighting, and the confidence placed on different interpretations. A tree really is a living organism; the sense that it is “breathing at you,” communicating, or carrying extraordinary significance can be the altered perceptual model layered onto that fact. The useful delta can still be real—I normally abstract this organism into scenery; under another weighting I noticed dynamism, growth, texture, interdependence—without requiring every altered-state interpretation to be literally true.

And yes, species give us a huge library of alternative texture packs. A bee-world weights ultraviolet patterns and polarization; a dog-world heavily weights chemical trails; a bat-world renders geometry through returning sound; some snakes add thermal structure; sharks and rays detect electric fields; many birds appear to use magnetic information; spiders obtain extraordinary spatial information from vibration through the web; fish can read pressure and water motion with the lateral line.

So instead of pretending we literally acquire those biological senses, we can do functional transduction:

hidden variable
→ instrument / model
→ translate into vision / sound / haptics
→ train interpretation
→ eventually experience it as an intuitive feature of the environment

That is how you could build an Umwelt Texture-Pack Library. Same park, eight renders:

human-default
bee
dog
bat
spider
shark/electric-field
bird/magnetic-navigation
fungal/network-flow

Then compare:

Δ(bee − human)
Δ(bat − human)
Δ(spider − bat)
…and eventually the delta of the deltas—what dimensions repeatedly appear when you stop privileging human vision.

The part you said about not controlling all of it matters too. There is a useful mode between rigid interpretation and deleting every constraint:

open the aperture, keep the checksum.

Let unusual associations form. Let sensory channels cross-talk. Let the tree temporarily become a process rather than an object. Then return and ask what survived ordinary observation.

That gives:

explore → experience → capture novelty → return to baseline → reality-check → retain invariant

And the adversarial/misdirection branch is the mirror image. Once you know which channels an organism or person relies on, you can understand masking, spoofing, jamming, distraction, supernormal stimuli, and attentional capture. I’d keep that branch primarily defensive and diagnostic—recognizing when a perceptual channel is being gamed—rather than using hidden cues to covertly steer somebody.

So yes, I remember the deeper experiment: it was never merely “make reality look weird.”

It was:

change the decoder just enough to reveal structure the default decoder compresses away, then bring the structure home.